  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A notable upper level ridge, currently centered\nover the Midwest, will shift east this week with anomalous \nheights overspreading the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas. Beneath \nthe ridge, an expansive area of anomalously warm low-level \nthicknesses are forecast to reside across the Southeast U.S., \nwith 850mb temperatures reaching around 20C. Meanwhile, \nsoutherly flow will help hold dewpoints in the 70s most days, \nespecially Wednesday through Friday. The combination of highs in\nthe 90s and dewpoints in the 70s will make it feel like 100 to \n110 degrees during that time. Forecast aids like the ECMWF's EFI\nshow an impressive signal for anomalous heat mid to late-week, \nadding confidence to the impact. Lastly, the NWS' experimental \nHeat Risk puts most of ENC in the \"Major\" impact category. \nBottom line, it will be dangerously hot and humid for much of \nthe upcoming week, with impacts expected to peak Wednesday- \nFriday. \n\nAssuming the forecast holds, heat headlines will likely be \nneeded for portions of the area. To give some context to the \nanomaly of this incoming heat wave, heat headlines a fairly rare\nthis late in the season. Since 2005, heat headlines have been \nissued in September only once (2020). If dewpoints mix out some,\nthen heat indices may not get as high, despite the anomalous \ntemps. Based on the latest guidance, though, headlines appear \nlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front is forecast to move through\nthe Carolinas Friday night or Saturday. At minimum, this will \nknock down the heat, bringing a refreshing break from the \nanomalous heat and humidity. Additionally, guidance show a \ndecent signal for convection with the front. Synoptically, the \nsetup appears favorable for strong to severe thunderstorms \ndepending on the timing of the passage of the front. Machine \nlearning guidance shows a decent signal for this potential as \nwell. From a probabilistic standpoint, the risk appears to be \nthe highest on Saturday.\n\nBehind the front, highs are forecast to drop back down into the\n80s, with lows in the 60s. Adding to this will be dewpoints \nfalling back into the 50s and 60s making it feel more \ncomfortable.",
